- op: discuss  # 4318
  at: '2026-09-11T02:55:03Z'  # 4318
  by: agent  # 4318
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4318
  clock: 4  # 4318
  nonce: '4318'  # 4318
  message: "DONE in PR #908 (merged e696785da4). Record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/kigub_09102026/ (PLAN.md pre-registered before any\noutcome number existed; Addenda A/B/C appended, never edited).\n\nTHIS ITEM'S OWN FILED REPRO, RE-RUN ROW BY ROW on phoenix-framework\n(`PYTHONHASHSEED=0`, both arms reproducing themselves exactly):\n\n    filed row         filed sites   edges BEFORE   edges AFTER\n    :ets.insert                 7              0             7\n    :ets.lookup                 2              0             2\n    :ets.new                    2              0             2\n    :ets.info                   1              0             1\n    :ets.delete                 1              0             1\n    :httpc.request              2              0             5\n\nFive of six match the filed site count exactly. `:httpc.request` returns 5\nagainst a filed 2 -- the filing named two call sites and there are five, the\nsame \"the item names one shape and the family has more\" pattern every cell in\nthis drain has hit.\n\nFIX. `_handle_dot_call` required the dot node to carry an `alias` child; an\natom module parses as `atom`. It now takes an atom receiver and emits straight\nto the unresolved fallback, with the LEADING COLON STRIPPED. The stripping is\nthe load-bearing half and is not cosmetic: `_module_matches` is component-aware\nand does no colon stripping, so `catalog='ets'` vs `hint=':ets'` is False --\nemitting the atom verbatim would have produced an L2 gain reaching no catalogue\nrow, which is precisely the failure INV-linub exists to catch. Executed before\nchoosing, not reasoned. It is also the cross-language parity answer: the erlang\nanalyzer emits `erlang:ets:0-0:insert:function` for the same OTP modules.\n\nAtom calls bypass the first-party resolution branches BY CONSTRUCTION, because\nthose end in the `any(module_dot in k ...)` substring test that WI-kafor files\nas a defect, and `\"ets.\"` is a substring of any project key like\n`Foo.Sockets.insert`. Pinned by a test.\n\nSCOPE SET BY PARITY WITH THE ALIAS PATH. A grammar probe found SIX\nearly-returning dot shapes, not the one filed. Fixed the one whose module is\nwritten literally at the call site; filed the rest as WI-taluz and pinned them\nas known gaps.\n\nMEASURED (full numbers in PLAN.md Addendum B/C):\n  analyze_elixir  11,100 -> 11,222 edges; APPEARED 122 over 25 OTP modules;\n                  VANISHED 0; symbols added 0, removed 0.\n  io-boundaries   total_io_edges 250 -> 277; distinct elixir primitives\n                  52 -> 65; LOST 0. db_read (0->8) and db_write (0->10) appear\n                  as boundary KINDS phoenix had never reached; all 18 chains\n                  have an `ets` sink.\n  verify-claims   findings 19 -> 31; two verdicts move inconclusive ->\n                  violated. ALL FIVE FINDINGS READ BACK AGAINST SOURCE:\n                  5/5 structurally correct, 0 wrong.\n\nTWO OF SIX PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS REFUTED, both recorded rather than\nsmoothed. (a) Edge count 122 against a floor of 150, chased to its cause:\n101 of the 103 unemitted sites are calls not inside a `def`, and a parity\nfixture shows the ALIAS path loses exactly the same ones -- filed as WI-dokib.\n(b) Predicted zero new findings; there were twelve.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT WAS WRONG BEFORE THE FIX WAS. The first A/B reported 32 VANISHED\nedges, which under this item's own pre-registration meant it does not ship.\nAttributed instead of accepted: the analyzer is non-deterministic across\nprocesses and disagrees with ITSELF by 67-166 edges. Filed as WI-jozap. Every\narm here pins the hash seed.\n\nALSO FIXED, incidental and disclosed: `io_primitives/elixir.yaml`'s header has\nasserted since 2026-04-15 (WI-vibur) that OTP is \"covered for the atom-access\npath\". True of the rows, false of the path, for nearly five months. The header\nnow says so with a date."  # 4318
